{"name":"Etruscan","hex":"#9b583c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Etruscan","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or pertaining to the region and culture of Etruria, a pre-Roman civilization in Italy."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An inhabitant of ancient Etruria."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Etruscan"}],"entries":[{"title":"Etruscan civilization","blocks":[{"text":"The Etruscans created a civilization in Etruria, in ancient Italy, with a common language and culture, and formed a federation of city-states. After adjacent lands had been conquered, its territory covered, at its greatest extent, roughly what is now Tuscany, western Umbria and northern Lazio, as well as what are now the Po Valley, Emilia-Romagna, south-eastern Lombardy, southern Veneto and western Campania."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Etruscan_civilization_map.png/330px-Etruscan_civilization_map.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Etruscan_civilization"},{"title":"Etruscan language","blocks":[{"text":"Etruscan was the language of the Etruscan civilization in the ancient region of Etruria, in Etruria Padana and Etruria Campana in what is now Italy. Etruscan influenced Latin but was eventually superseded by it. Around 13,000 Etruscan inscriptions have been found so far, only a small minority of which are of significant length; some bilingual inscriptions with texts also in Latin, Greek, or Phoenician; and a few dozen purported loanwords. It is attested from 700 BC to 50 AD and the relation of Etruscan to other languages has been a source of long-running speculation and study. Nowadays it is generally agreed to be in the Tyrrhenian language family but it used commonly to be treated as an isolate, although there were also a number of other lesser-known hypotheses."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Perugia%2C_Museo_archeologico_Nazionale_dell%27Umbria%2C_cippo_di_Perugia.jpg/330px-Perugia%2C_Museo_archeologico_Nazionale_dell%27Umbria%2C_cippo_di_Perugia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Etruscan_language"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Tular_Rasnal_1.jpg/500px-Tular_Rasnal_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Tular_Rasnal_1.jpg/500px-Tular_Rasnal_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Boundary stone from Cortona\nEtruscan: Tular RasnalEnglish: Boundary of the People","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATular_Rasnal_1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Exekias_Dionysos_Staatliche_Antikensammlungen_2044.jpg/500px-Exekias_Dionysos_Staatliche_Antikensammlungen_2044.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Exekias_Dionysos_Staatliche_Antikensammlungen_2044.jpg/1280px-Exekias_Dionysos_Staatliche_Antikensammlungen_2044.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Exekias_Dionysos_Staatliche_Antikensammlungen_2044.jpg/500px-Exekias_Dionysos_Staatliche_Antikensammlungen_2044.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Dionysus Cup, a kylix painted by the Athenian Exekias ca. 530 BCE, showing the narrative of Dionysus's capture by Tyrrhenian pirates and transfiguration of them into dolphins in the seventh Homeric Hymn[47]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AExekias_Dionysos_Staatliche_Antikensammlungen_2044.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Urne_cin%C3%A9raire_imitant_u